author_en="Heojeong Yoon (Director, Institute for Human & Future; Professor, Department of Science Education, Chuncheon National University of Education)",
summary_en="Marking the opening of the Institute for Human & Future, this essay asks anew how we should define the human in the age of artificial intelligence. Facing problems that technology alone cannot solve, such as inequality and the climate crisis, it examines the role of university researchers and the need for interdisciplinary collaboration, and proposes three directions for universities and research.",
quote_en="The future is something we choose before it is something we predict. Which future we choose cannot be decided by technological efficiency alone; it must take into account human dignity, justice in our communities, and coexistence with nature.",
tags_en="#HumanAndFuture #ArtificialIntelligence #ConceptOfTheHuman #InterdisciplinaryResearch #EducationPolicy #ClimateCrisis",
body_en="""The Institute for Human & Future has opened its doors. Researchers in science education, the humanities, the social sciences, education, and science and technology have come together to explore the human values that endure in an era of rapid technological change, and to connect what we learn to the future of education and society. On the occasion of our opening, I would like to share why we are turning to this subject now, and where I believe university research should be heading.

## How Should We Define the Human in the Age of AI?

Artificial intelligence now writes and draws, and it has taken over a considerable share of tasks that call for judgment. Research on brain–computer interfaces (BCIs), which connect the brain directly to computers, is also advancing quickly, steadily blurring the boundary between humans and machines. Faced with these changes, we find ourselves confronting once again an age-old question: What is a human being?

The international community shares this concern. In its report, the International Commission on the Futures of Education, established by UNESCO, redefined the purpose of education as enhancing and protecting human dignity and capabilities in our relationships with each other and with the planet (International Commission on the Futures of Education, 2021). UNESCO's guidance on generative AI in education and research likewise adopts, as its guiding principle, a human-centred approach that protects human agency (Miao & Holmes, 2023). In Korea, the Framework Act on the Development of Artificial Intelligence and the Creation of a Foundation for Trust took effect in January 2026, establishing an institutional framework that addresses both the development of AI and trust in it (Framework Act on the Development of Artificial Intelligence and the Creation of a Foundation for Trust, 2026).

For a long time, we have defined human beings by the abilities that set us apart from other beings: humans think, use language, and make tools. But now that machines perform many of these functions, abilities alone can no longer explain what it is to be human. From now on, we must consider not only what humans can do, but also what we value and how we live together. Values such as dignity and autonomy, relationships and empathy, will be our starting point. This is why the Institute has chosen "AI and Other Emerging Technologies: Redefining the Concept of the Human" as its theme for the first year.

This question bears directly on education. The OECD Learning Compass 2030, which places student agency at its center and identifies creating new value, reconciling tensions and dilemmas, and taking responsibility as the transformative competencies students need to shape a better future, reflects the same concern (OECD, 2019). In Korea, the Ministry of Education announced in its 2023 Digital-Based Education Innovation Plan that AI digital textbooks would be introduced from 2025 (Ministry of Education, 2023). Amid controversy over schools' lack of readiness and the pace of the rollout, however, an amendment passed by the National Assembly in August 2025 changed their legal status from textbooks to educational materials (Cho, 2025). The case shows that before asking how quickly to adopt a technology, we must first ask what that technology means for learning. What students need is not the ability to answer faster than machines, but the agency to decide for themselves where to use technology and to take responsibility for the results.

## Inequality and the Climate Crisis: Problems Technology Alone Cannot Solve

The benefits of technology are not shared equally. The World Economic Forum estimates that by 2030, job creation and displacement due to structural labour-market transformation will amount to 22% of today's total jobs, with 170 million new jobs created and 92 million displaced (World Economic Forum, 2025). The costs of this transition fall first on people and regions with fewer opportunities for retraining. As the gaps widen between those who gain early access to new tools and those who do not, and between regions with strong educational conditions and those without, the digital divide, the education gap, and inequalities in jobs and income can readily turn into conflicts between generations, social classes, and regions. The same technology can affect society in very different ways depending on whom it serves and how it is used.

The climate crisis must be seen in the same light. The Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC) has found that vulnerable communities who have historically contributed the least to current climate change are disproportionately affected, and that approximately 3.3 to 3.6 billion people live in contexts that are highly vulnerable to climate change (IPCC, 2023). To reflect on anthropocentrism is not to diminish the worth of human beings; it is to rethink the conditions under which humans can continue to live sustainably with nature. The 2022 Revised National Curriculum, announced in December 2022, reflects this awareness: it cites responses to both the digital transformation and climate and ecological change as the background to the revision (Ministry of Education, 2022a, 2022b).

Technological, social, and environmental problems are intertwined. New technologies create new divides, and the damage caused by the climate crisis falls first on the most vulnerable groups. It is therefore difficult to find solutions by addressing any one dimension in isolation.

## The Role of University Researchers

Until now, discussions of the future have centered on what technology can do. Higher education policy is no exception. Since 2025, the Ministry of Education has placed its emphasis on developing talent that meets industrial and technological demand, so that universities can become hubs of regional innovation. It supports the development of basic AI curricula at universities (Ministry of Education, 2026a) and has made AI education a core priority (Ministry of Education, 2026b). These policies are all necessary, yet questions about what technology should be used for, and what we must protect amid change, have received comparatively little attention. Answering them is a task for the humanities and social sciences, which have long studied humanity and society, and for education, which raises the next generation, working together with science and technology.

The university is a place where we can step back from immediate interests and examine problems from a long-term perspective. University researchers should be the first to raise questions that society has not yet asked, and to build the evidence for them with care. The deeper social conflicts become, the more valuable it is to offer verified evidence and balanced perspectives.

## Finding Policy Agendas through Interdisciplinary Collaboration

Today's problems cannot be solved with the knowledge of a single field. A way forward appears only when engineering and philosophy address AI ethics together, when economics and education tackle the technology divide, and when the natural and social sciences take on the climate crisis together. This is why the Institute pursues interdisciplinary research that brings together researchers from many fields.

Long-term educational planning at the national level is also gathering pace. The National Education Commission is preparing the National Education Development Plan for 2028–2037 and is promoting a public deliberation process on reforming the college admissions system (Hwang, 2026). At this moment, when the direction of education for the next ten years is being set, evidence built jointly across disciplines must inform the policy debate.

The Institute has designated 2026–2035 as its first cycle and will carry on its research under the academic theme "Human & Future." The first three years (2026–2028) are devoted to Forecasting Future Change, the next three (2029–2031) to Exploring Future Challenges, and the following three (2032–2034) to the Transition to the Future. Each stage cycles through three perspectives—technology, the humanities and society, and the environment—year by year, and in 2035 we will synthesize the results of the first cycle and set the direction for the next decade. In the process, we aim to identify policy agendas to prepare for future society and to propose practicable solutions.

[사진 FIG | Research directions of the Institute for Human & Future's first cycle (2026–2035)]

## Directions for Universities and Research

Finally, I would like to propose three directions that universities and research should take in an era of technological transformation.

First, university research should devote more effort to questioning the direction of technology than to keeping pace with its speed. Research on how to use new technologies must go hand in hand with research that examines what those technologies mean for people and communities.

Second, we need an institutional foundation so that collaboration across disciplines does not end as a one-off event. Knowledge from different fields can truly meet only when there are joint research projects, joint curricula, and research evaluation systems that properly recognize interdisciplinary work.

Third, we must widen the channels through which research findings reach classrooms, local communities, and policymaking. In particular, universities of education and colleges of education, which prepare future teachers, should bring reflection on technology and humanity into teacher education, so that these concerns carry through to schools.

The future is something we choose before it is something we predict. Which future we choose cannot be decided by technological efficiency alone; it must take into account human dignity, justice in our communities, and coexistence with nature. Laying the groundwork for that judgment is the task now before universities and researchers.""",
