body_en="""## It Is the Fastest Route, So Why Does It Feel Wrong?

> I enter my destination into the navigation system. A blue line appears on the screen almost at once, along with a notice that I will arrive ten minutes earlier than usual. It is the “fastest route” the machine has calculated.
> Following the directions, I leave the main road and turn into a narrow alley. On one side, a daycare van is parked, and children are climbing out of it. On the other, an elderly person leaning on a cane is walking past my car. Without realizing it, I slow down. “In 50 meters, turn left,” the navigation system says calmly. All the way down the alley, I cannot shake the feeling that something is wrong.

What was the problem? The machine found exactly the “fastest route” it had been asked for. “Optimal” always presupposes a question: optimal for whom, and by what standard? Yet we often hand over the work of setting that standard to machines without a second thought. Perhaps the problem lay not in the machine’s answer, but in the fact that we never asked ourselves, “What makes a route a good one?”

## Why a Science Educator Is Turning to the Humanities and Social Sciences

This question leads me to revisit the long-standing goals of science education. Its main aim has been to help students understand scientific concepts accurately, analyze data, and develop the ability to test hypotheses through experiments. In today’s environment, where machines have a clear comparative advantage in transmitting and processing knowledge, this aim alone is no longer enough.

A more fundamental problem is that many hard questions, such as how far gene editing should be allowed, the debate over nuclear power and renewable energy, and the biases of generative AI, require scientific understanding as a necessary condition, yet cannot be settled by it alone. What such moments demand is a judgment not about “what is possible” but about “what we ought to do.” Science education has tended to be built around problems with clear answers, leaving issues that have no single right answer outside its scope. As a science educator, I reflect with regret that we have not adequately addressed the gap between what is “scientifically correct” and what is “socially desirable,” and I now turn to the humanities and social sciences to help cultivate the capacity to ask the right questions and to decide together.

## The Age of AI: Humans with Integrative Intelligence

Starting from this concern, I propose “integrative intelligence” as the core concept for the kind of person that education in the age of AI should aim to develop. Integrative intelligence is the ability to understand complex problems from the perspectives of different systems of knowledge, to make judgments, and to put those judgments into practice. It is distinct from mere convergence, because it is formed not by adding up knowledge from different disciplines but through a process in which, faced with a real problem, each perspective illuminates and corrects the others. One might think, for example, of the humanities re-examining the possibilities that science presents from the standpoint of values, and of the social sciences turning them into rules and institutions that a community can accept.

AI will become steadily better at processing scientific knowledge. Setting goals, judging values, taking responsibility for outcomes, and reaching agreement as a community, however, remain ours, not because AI is unable to perform them, but because they are domains that must not be delegated to AI.

Integrative intelligence rests on three pillars. Scientific literacy is the ability to understand natural phenomena and to reason rationally on the basis of evidence. Only by understanding what data and algorithms a navigation system uses to calculate a route do we have grounds to trust, or to doubt, the machine’s answer. Humanistic reflection is the ability to question human dignity, the meaning of life, and ethical values. The instinct that made me slow down in an alley where children and older people were passing belongs here. Social participation is the ability to understand social structures and diversity and to take part responsibly in addressing the problems of the community. The standard for what is “optimal” cannot be set unilaterally by an individual; it requires a process of building consensus that reconciles the differing interests of residents and drivers, and of local governments and the national government.

The direction that runs through all three pillars is future orientation. It looks ahead to technological and social change, guides responsible choices for the next generation, and leads us to ask how today’s shortest route will affect the local community ten years from now.

[사진 FIG | Figure 1. The pillar structure of integrative intelligence]

## Implications for Schools and Policy

Cultivating integrative intelligence calls for three changes in schools. First, science classes should go beyond explaining concepts and checking answers, and give students experience in examining evidence, comparing values, and drawing conclusions on issues that have no single right answer. Second, humanities and social studies classes should give students experience, as citizens with scientific and technological literacy, in framing questions, reading context, taking others’ perspectives, and making judgments under uncertainty. Third, the teacher’s role should shift from transmitter of right answers to designer of questions and facilitator of discussion, and AI should be used not as a tool that generates answers but as a thinking partner that challenges students’ reasoning and exposes its weak points.

For example, a teacher might bring the “shortest route” proposed by AI into class and have students discuss: What data is this route based on? Who lives along it? What standard should we choose? Students can then treat the AI’s answer not as the right answer but as a starting point for discussion, revising it and arriving at a proposal they agree on. Such lessons require cross-subject collaboration in which science teachers and humanities and social studies teachers design a single problem together, supported institutionally by measures such as dedicated time for joint lesson planning and teacher training on issue-based teaching.

## Education That Raises People Who Ask What Makes a Good Route

Collaboration between science education and the humanities and social sciences is not simply a way to offset the side effects of science and technology. Nor does it diminish the role of science. It completes the place that science holds in society. What this collaboration aims for is to raise citizens who have a sense of direction as well as technical competence, members of an educational community who think together across disciplinary boundaries, and people who create good questions and consensus rather than merely finding right answers.

In the face of machines that find routes for us, what we must teach is the capacity to ask, “What makes a route a good one?” A machine can find the route for us. But asking whether that route is right, and whom it is for, is a task that belongs to people. Raising people who ask such questions is the work of education.""",
